From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 23:30:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 03/37] LLHD Sig2Reg: narrow drive intervals through mux trees

tryNarrowArrayInjectChain/tryNarrowBitConcatChain each require the
pass-through shape to sit directly on the feeder, which covers a hold
expressed as a clock-enable self-feedback mux (unwrapClockEnable peels that
first). It does not cover RTL that expresses the hold by re-injecting into the
signal's own probe on every arm:

    %58 = comb.mux %57, %53, %55              // no self-feedback arm
    %53 = hw.array_inject %74[%false], %52    // element 0
    %55 = comb.mux %54, %51, %50              // ... also element 0

Every leaf writes the same element, so the drive's real interval is that
element -- but with a mux on top neither helper matches, the drive is reported
at full width, and two sibling drives of disjoint elements then look like they
collide. On OpenC910 that rejected 65 signals and blackboxed 55 modules.

tryNarrowFeeder recurses into both mux arms and unions the results. Sound
because the union is an upper bound on what either arm writes and the caller
reconstructs each range from the register's own RESULT, so whichever arm fired
the result holds that range's value. Any arm that cannot be narrowed forces the
whole feeder back to full width, and overlapping-but-unequal ranges between
arms are refused rather than merged -- this stays a pure narrowing of the
existing conservative answer, so it can only admit promotions the old code
rejected, never the reverse.

OpenC910: signals refused as overlapping 65 -> 38, residual llhd ops 471 ->
271, modules still holding LLHD 35 of 503. The 38 that remain are genuinely
ambiguous -- the same signal driven at two granularities (5 x 2-bit and
several 1-bit slices of one i10), which do overlap.

ibex unaffected: core.mlir, core_deagg.mlir and struct.mlir all byte-identical.
